# Code owners. GitHub auto-requests a review from the owner of every path a pull request touches.
#
# Two things to know, because CODEOWNERS is easy to mis-trust:
#   1. It only takes effect for the copy on the repository's DEFAULT branch.
#   2. On its own it *requests* review, it does not require it. The enforcement is the
#      "Require review from Code Owners" setting in the branch ruleset - without that, this file is
#      advisory only.
#
# The per-path entries below are deliberately redundant with the `*` rule while there is one maintainer.
# They exist so that the day `*` gains a second owner, the supply-chain surface stays narrowly owned
# instead of silently widening with it. Last matching rule wins, so order matters.

*                       @louisbrulenaudet

# The CI harness. `uses:` refs are pinned by commit SHA, and these workflows carry the only proof in the
# repository that the runtime image's hardening actually holds (`ops/ci`) - a change here is a
# supply-chain change, not a config tweak.
/.github/               @louisbrulenaudet

# Build and dependency surface. `uv.lock` is canonical and `requirements.txt` is the file GitHub's
# dependency graph reads, so a change to either moves what security alerts are computed against.
/Dockerfile             @louisbrulenaudet
/.dockerignore          @louisbrulenaudet
/compose.yaml           @louisbrulenaudet
/make/                  @louisbrulenaudet
/pyproject.toml         @louisbrulenaudet
/uv.lock                @louisbrulenaudet
/requirements.txt       @louisbrulenaudet

# Agent harness. Rules, skills and hooks change how every future agent behaves in this repository, and
# `hooks/` can block or allow git operations outright.
/AGENTS.md              @louisbrulenaudet
/CLAUDE.md              @louisbrulenaudet
/.claude/               @louisbrulenaudet
/.cursor/               @louisbrulenaudet
/hooks/                 @louisbrulenaudet
